{"issue":{"title":"Add a jsonl-lint tool that reports every row of a JSONL file that is not valid JSON","problem":"The repo has no tool for checking JSONL files. `packages/` does not exist yet (README: \"No tools yet: `packages/` is empty, and `npm test` says so and passes\"), and nothing in the repo checks a JSONL file row by row. When a journal kept as JSONL gets a torn or hand-edited row, the only way to find it today is to have a reader fail on it.","reason":"Journals are kept as JSONL. One bad row breaks every reader, and the reader's error usually doesn't say which line is bad. A standalone lint tool that lists every bad row by line number and exits non-zero lets a person or a script find the damage and stop on it before a reader trips.","outcome":"After `npm ci` at the repo root, `npx jsonl-lint <file>` checks each row of the file as JSON. It prints one line for every invalid row, with the file path and the 1-based line number, and exits 0 only when every row is valid. A missing file or a missing argument is an error with a non-zero exit. The tool is its own npm workspace under `packages/`, has its own `node:test` suite that the root `npm test` runs, and the README lists it.","expect":["After `npm ci` at the repo root, `npx jsonl-lint` resolves to the new tool. A file whose rows are all valid JSON, and which ends with a trailing newline, exits 0 and prints no `<path>:<n>:` line.","Rows that are valid JSON but not objects (e.g. `42`, `\"s\"`, `[1,2]`, `null`) are accepted as valid.","For a file with invalid rows at lines 2, 4 and 6 (including a torn last row with no trailing newline), `npx jsonl-lint <file>` exits non-zero. Its combined stdout+stderr contains exactly one line starting with `<file>:2:`, one starting with `<file>:4:` and one starting with `<file>:6:`, and no line starting with `<file>:1:`, `<file>:3:` or `<file>:5:`.","An empty file (0 bytes) exits 0.","`npx jsonl-lint <path>` with a path that does not exist exits non-zero, prints a message containing that path, and prints no Node stack trace (no line starting with whitespace followed by `at `).","`npx jsonl-lint` with no file argument exits non-zero.","The tool is a new npm workspace under `packages/` (e.g. `packages/jsonl-lint/package.json`) whose `bin` maps `jsonl-lint` to an ES-module entry file, with no `dependencies` field or an empty one, and no build step.","The new workspace has `node:test` tests covering an all-valid file, multiple invalid rows with correct line numbers, and a missing file. `npm test` at the repo root runs them (its output shows tests from the new workspace) and exits 0.","The `test` GitHub Actions check passes on the pull request's head commit.","README.md no longer says `packages/` is empty or that there are no tools. Its \"What is here\" section lists jsonl-lint with a one-line description and how to run it."],"proofs":[{"line":"After `npm ci` at the repo root, `npx jsonl-lint` resolves to the new tool. A file whose rows are all valid JSON, and which ends with a trailing newline, exits 0 and prints no `<path>:<n>:` line.","proof":"run","how":"npm ci && printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":[1,2]}\\n{\"c\":\"x\"}\\n' > /tmp/ok.jsonl && npx jsonl-lint /tmp/ok.jsonl > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo exit=$?; grep -c '^/tmp/ok.jsonl:[0-9]*:' /tmp/out.txt  # expect exit=0 and count 0"},{"line":"Rows that are valid JSON but not objects (e.g. `42`, `\"s\"`, `[1,2]`, `null`) are accepted as valid.","proof":"run","how":"printf '42\\n\"s\"\\n[1,2]\\nnull\\n' > /tmp/scalars.jsonl && npx jsonl-lint /tmp/scalars.jsonl; echo exit=$?  # expect exit=0"},{"line":"For a file with invalid rows at lines 2, 4 and 6 (including a torn last row with no trailing newline), `npx jsonl-lint <file>` exits non-zero. Its combined stdout+stderr contains exactly one line starting with `<file>:2:`, one starting with `<file>:4:` and one starting with `<file>:6:`, and no line starting with `<file>:1:`, `<file>:3:` or `<file>:5:`.","proof":"run","how":"printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{bad\\n{\"b\":2}\\n{\"c\": 3,}\\n[1]\\n{\"torn\":\"x' > /tmp/bad.jsonl && npx jsonl-lint /tmp/bad.jsonl > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo exit=$?; for n in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do echo \"$n: $(grep -c \"^/tmp/bad.jsonl:$n:\" /tmp/out.txt)\"; done  # expect exit non-zero; counts 1:0 2:1 3:0 4:1 5:0 6:1"},{"line":"An empty file (0 bytes) exits 0.","proof":"run","how":": > /tmp/empty.jsonl && npx jsonl-lint /tmp/empty.jsonl; echo exit=$?  # expect exit=0"},{"line":"`npx jsonl-lint <path>` with a path that does not exist exits non-zero, prints a message containing that path, and prints no Node stack trace (no line starting with whitespace followed by `at `).","proof":"run","how":"npx jsonl-lint /tmp/does-not-exist-102.jsonl > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo exit=$?; grep -c 'does-not-exist-102.jsonl' /tmp/out.txt; grep -cE '^\\s+at ' /tmp/out.txt  # expect exit non-zero, first count >=1, second count 0"},{"line":"`npx jsonl-lint` with no file argument exits non-zero.","proof":"run","how":"npx jsonl-lint < /dev/null; echo exit=$?  # expect exit non-zero"},{"line":"The tool is a new npm workspace under `packages/` (e.g. `packages/jsonl-lint/package.json`) whose `bin` maps `jsonl-lint` to an ES-module entry file, with no `dependencies` field or an empty one, and no build step.","proof":"read","how":"Read the new package.json under packages/: check that `bin.jsonl-lint` (or a string `bin` with name jsonl-lint) points to a .js/.mjs file that exists, that the package has \"type\": \"module\" or the entry is .mjs, that `dependencies` is absent or {}, and that no build script is needed to run it. Confirm with `npm query .workspace` that it is listed as a workspace."},{"line":"The new workspace has `node:test` tests covering an all-valid file, multiple invalid rows with correct line numbers, and a missing file. `npm test` at the repo root runs them (its output shows tests from the new workspace) and exits 0.","proof":"run","how":"npm ci && npm test; echo exit=$?  # expect exit=0 and test output from the jsonl-lint workspace (not 'no workspaces under packages/'); then read the test file(s) to confirm they import from 'node:test' and cover the three cases"},{"line":"The `test` GitHub Actions check passes on the pull request's head commit.","proof":"ci","how":"Read the `test` check (workflow .github/workflows/test.yml) on the PR head commit: conclusion success."},{"line":"README.md no longer says `packages/` is empty or that there are no tools. Its \"What is here\" section lists jsonl-lint with a one-line description and how to run it.","proof":"read","how":"Read README.md at the PR head: the 'No tools yet' bullet is gone or rewritten, and the 'What is here' section has an entry for jsonl-lint that describes it and shows how to run it."}],"non_goals":["Schema validation of rows, e.g. requiring objects or specific keys. Only JSON syntax is checked.","Repairing or rewriting the file (no --fix).","Reading from stdin, checking several files or globs in one call, or watching files.","Publishing the package to npm, or wiring it into the harness in suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine.","Changing any harness spec."],"assumptions":["The command name is `jsonl-lint`, from the request. It lives as its own workspace under `packages/` (the repo's one-folder-per-tool rule) and is exposed through package.json `bin`, so `npx jsonl-lint` works from the repo root after `npm ci`.","Output format: each invalid row is reported on its own line starting with `<path as given>:<1-based line number>:`, followed by a short reason. This is the common linter convention and makes the line number unambiguous. The request only asked for the line number, so the reason text is free.","Line numbers are 1-based and count every physical line separated by `\\n`.","A single trailing newline at the end of the file does not create an extra row, and an empty file has no rows, so it is valid. A blank or whitespace-only line elsewhere is not valid JSON and is reported. That case is left out of the expect lines.","A trailing `\\r` (CRLF files) is treated as JSON whitespace, as JSON.parse already does.","'Valid JSON' means anything JSON.parse accepts, scalars and arrays included. Whether a row is an object is not checked.","Exit codes: 0 when all rows are valid; non-zero for invalid rows, a missing file, an unreadable path or a missing argument. Using distinct codes (e.g. 1 for invalid rows, 2 for usage or I/O errors) is allowed but not required.","Errors such as a missing file are reported as a one-line message naming the path, not as an uncaught exception with a stack trace."]}}